WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Before we get started today, I'd like to ask you to take a moment to fill out our listener survey. We want to learn more about you, what you think of this podcast, and what topics you'd like us to cover. So after you listen to this episode, please go to our website, www.spinkingofpsychology.org, and look for the link to our listener survey. There will also be a link in the show notes for this episode. Thanks in advance for taking our little poll, and now on to the episode. In February, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention released data showing that teen girls in the U.S. are engulfed in what they called a wave of sadness and violence. The numbers are startling.

2
00:00:43.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Nearly one in three teen girls has seriously considered suicide. 57% of teen girls said they felt persistently sad or hopeless. And 18% had experienced sexual violence in the prior year. The report also found extremely high levels of distress among LGBTQ plus teens. More than one in five of them had attempted suicide in the prior year, and more than half reported poor mental health. Today we're going to dig into those numbers. What is behind this crisis in teen mental health?

3
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Are we just seeing the effects of the pandemic? Is it social media? Something else? Why are girls suffering so much more than boys? Which teens are more at risk? And what can parents, peers, schools, and communities do to help teens cope and stay safe and healthy in the face of such widespread distress? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills.

4
00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:39.000
My guest today is Dr. Kathleen Ethier, director of the CDC's Division of Adolescent and School Health, which put out the new report. She has been at the CDC for more than 20 years, leading research and policy work on health issues affecting youth and women. Before joining the CDC, she spent six years on the research faculty at Yale University, working on studies of HIV, STDs, and unplanned pregnancy prevention. Dr. ETHier holds a PhD in Social Psychology from the Graduate Center of the City University of New York. Thank you for joining me today, Dr. ETHier. Hi, so glad to be here. Let's start with the numbers I mentioned in the introduction. Almost one-third of teen girls said they have considered suicide, almost three and five, felt persistently said, and hopeless, and 18% had experienced sexual violence.

5
00:02:39.000 --> 00:03:10.000
I think most members of the public who have heard these numbers were alarmed. And as someone who's been working in the field for a long time, what about you? Were you surprised? Were you alarmed? Yeah, obviously, yes. we you know the euthress behavior survey which is the data that um which the report that we released is is part not all but part of the data um that's contained in the uterus behavior survey you know the survey has been around for more than 30 years um and as an adolescent health

6
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:50.000
um my background is in adolescent health i've been studying adolescent health for many many years and so we use the wirebS all the time it's pretty standard or pretty standard way of understanding the health and well-being of young people in the country. And so I think, you know, we had been seeing prior to the pandemic, we'd been seeing mental health move in the wrong direction. We had seen that kind of centered around female students and LGBTQ plus students. And then during the pandemic, we had additional confirmation that youth mental health was in crisis, that teenage girls seemed to be at higher risk.

7
00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:44.000
So we saw some data from emergency room visits indicating that they were more likely to be showing up during the pandemic with having attempted suicides. We had some inklings. I think we were not, we weren't prepared, I would say, for the consistency across all of the different measures that we included in the report. And then also some of the 10-year trends that we've been monitoring for, you know, kind of the past number of years. And so, you know, we include these individual measures in this report around sexual behavior, substance use, experience of violence and mental health and suicide. Because for us in the Division of Adolescent School, help it speaks to the programmatic work. We know that these factors are interconnected. Most of these variables, we've included, this is the third time we've done this particular report.

8
00:04:44.000 --> 00:05:38.000
We include them every single time. We have some shifts to make sure we're being relevant. And so we didn't look at the data really before we decided to have that these were the set of variables we were going to include in this report. So when we put it all together and looked at the data and looked kind of across, we were not expecting that for all of the substance use measures that we include in the report. And it's not every substance use measure that's in the wire BS, but for the measures that we included in this report, for almost all of the experiences of violence except for, being threatened or injured with a weapon, and across all of the mental health and suicidal thoughts and behavior data we included in this report, that girls would be so significantly at such a significant increase compared to boys. And that was just really consistent,

9
00:05:38.000 --> 00:06:27.000
and I think we were not expecting that. You know, we've been monitoring kind of in these 10-year chunks some of these variables like the proportion of youth who had been forced to have sex. And so when we, in 2017, when we looked at that 10-year trend in 2019, when we looked at that 10-year trend, there were no changes over those 10-year periods in that variable. When we looked at the 2011 to 2021 data, that was the first time we'd really seen an increase in that measure, that trend measure. And so that was really also very alarming to us. you know, we've been saying for a while that, that, you know, out of every 10 teenage girls that you know, at least one has been raped. And that has been consistent for the number of years that we've been looking at these 10-year trends.

10
00:06:27.000 --> 00:07:00.000
And that changed. So, yes, we were very alarmed. We look to our programmatic work to see how we can help. And we do have school-based strategies that address some of these issues, which I know we'll talk about today. So I think, you know, you see the data. It is extremely alarming. It is our public health duty to get the data out and talked about and make it made accessible, which, you know, we've been trying to do. The next question, as you mentioned, is what do we, why?

11
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:38.000
Why is this? Why are we seeing this data? And then the next question after that is what do we do about it? With girls in particular and just the fact that these numbers are so alarming, do you have any way of knowing what? whether this is just a question of they're more willing to tell the truth on polls than they had been in prior years. Might that be a factor that there's more openness about this kind of conversation now? I think that I think it's very likely that we are doing a better job giving young people the language to describe their mental health. I don't know that it's less about telling the truth.

12
00:07:38.000 --> 00:08:36.000
This is an anonymous survey and it's always been an anonymous survey. So I think, you know, perhaps there is less stigma, you know, about talking about mental health. But I also do think that we in a really positive way are, have been giving young people the language to say what's happening with them. What concerns me is that while we're giving them the language to talk to describe their mental health on an understanding. anonymous survey, what I worry about is that we aren't giving them the pathways to tell adults, whether that's their parents or their school counselors or their teachers or a mental health professional, you know, or their primary care doctor. That we're not given, that we, I'm, not sure that data doesn't tell us that we've given them the pathway to, to get help. And that

13
00:08:36.000 --> 00:09:26.000
worries me. Before we get too much further into this, I wanted to ask you about the cohort, the data, how you do this, how many kids are involved, how is it nationally representative? How do you pull it all together and how are these youth basically approached and polled? So this is a school-based survey, so this is a survey of 9th through 12 graders. And this year, the sample size was over. Over 17,000 young people collected in schools across the country. Again, we've been doing this survey for more than 30 years. It is part of a system of surveys. So the data that I'm talking with you about that we released in this report is part of a national survey.

14
00:09:26.000 --> 00:10:18.000
There are also 47 state surveys and 28 local surveys. So there are across the country, there are lots of individual youth risk behavior surveys that are conducted. And so we can, we collect this data in conjunction with the states and locals as well. So we, we work with them so that we're, you know, we're not overtaxing any individual school because they appear in either the national or the state sample. We, we use a sampling frame and it's probably somewhat complicated, but we use a sampling frame that allows us to use the demographics of the school, of the individual school to make sure that the data is nationally representative. It doesn't mean that data from every, it doesn't include data from every state,

15
00:10:18.000 --> 00:11:10.000
but it is nationally representative. And we do a lot of, we do a lot of like non-biased testing to make sure that if, you know, we get data from a school. And then we have a different school that says, no, we really don't want you to come in, that there isn't kind of systematic differences between schools that agree to participate in schools that don't. So there's a lot of methodological and analytic support that we use in the sample to make sure that it is nationally representative. For all the data geeks who listen to your podcast. And I'm sure there are some. So I have to ask you about social media because there's been so much public and media attention recently to how social media can harm teens' mental health.

16
00:11:10.000 --> 00:12:17.000
Do you think at least some of the blame for what we're seeing in this survey lies there? You know, I think it's, I think it is more complicated than it would, honestly, it would be wonderful to have kind of a silver bullet that if we just fix that one thing, then this would all get better. I don't think that that's the case, although I think that social media does come with significant risks and the potential for significant harm. But it also comes with a lot of opportunity for connection. And it comes with a lot of opportunity for young people to access information that they may not have any other way of accessing. And so it's not the kind of thing that I think we want to kind of throw it. out without really kind of trying to figure out what should we keep what's important for young people to have and and then what should we how should we try to protect them I think and this is

17
00:12:17.000 --> 00:13:11.000
where social media probably plays in I think a lot of what's underlying this is social isolation you know we as humans and I think you know the folks listening to your podcast who are who are their psychologists themselves are interested in psychology. I think we know, and as a social psychologist myself, we know that as humans, we are social beings. We require social interaction. And many of the things that we're looking at in this report, both in terms of, I think, substance use and experience of violence and mental health and suicidality, have some component of social isolation as a cause underneath. And so I think with, and I think that's, you know, partially why the pandemic was so impactful was because we were separated from each other. And young

18
00:13:11.000 --> 00:14:05.000
people lost the supports that they have in school. They lost their peer groups for periods of time. And I think that was really impactful. And I think we on the opposite side see the power of connectedness in protecting the health and well-being of young people. And so, So where social media comes in, I think, is that that can become a substitute connector. And so can provide some connection. What I'm not sure about is whether it provides real connection in a way that protects health and well-being. And if not, how do we shift young people to connections that are more fulfilling? and support them in ways that the connection that they're getting through social media probably

19
00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:37.000
doesn't. Yeah. Some of the really shocking numbers that came out of this report were for girls and LGBTQ plus teens. And I say LGBTQ without the T because I know you didn't ask about gender identity this time. Right. Why do you think these two groups were faring so much worse than boys or at least than straight boys? and how were boys doing? So, I mean, in many ways, no, especially around mental health and suicide, we saw increases

20
00:14:37.000 --> 00:15:17.000
across every, across both boys and girls across every racial and ethnic group that we included. And in this report, we actually included seven categories of race ethnicity, which we hadn't done before. And I think neither LGBTQ plus youth or straight youth were doing particularly well. mental health. I mean, 40% overall, 40% of the students in the sample said that they felt so persistently sad and hopeless for at least two weeks in the last year that they couldn't do the regular activities, which, you know, you will all recognize as depressive symptoms. And so,

21
00:15:17.000 --> 00:16:08.000
you know, that I just, I do want to say that, you know, it's not like, you know, girls were doing terrible and boys were doing so much better. I mean, girls were doing significantly worse than boys and LGBTQ LGBTQ LGBTQ plus students were doing significantly worse than than heterosexual youth most of the almost consistently in the same way that we saw in this report for girls LGBTQ plus youth had already been significantly doing significantly worse than straight youth prior to the pandemic so we had really consistently been seeing that data since 2015, which is when we started asking about sexual identity. So, and we don't have trends on this population for this year because we changed the question

22
00:16:08.000 --> 00:17:11.000
to be better reflective of sexual identity. And so we were, we were less surprised because we had been seeing it, but nowhere less concerned that a quarter, for instance, a quarter of LGBTQ plus students attempted suicide in the year prior to the survey. That's really high, you know, to think about a quarter of those students didn't, you know, attempted to not be here anymore. Like that just, you know, that is just really devastating. I think as much as we've made significant strides in positive support for LGBTQ plus students, there's still a long way to go. And I think, you know, the conversations, the public conversations over the last few years

23
00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:58.000
around, certainly around trans youth, which again, we haven't, we didn't include that question in this sample. But it affects, it affects, you know, all young people in that way. And so I think we would be naive to think that those conversations are not impacting them. I mean, we do have, you know, earlier studies that have shown that in states prior to marriage equality becoming available across the country, when we looked at states that supported marriage equality versus those that did not, we saw significant differences in suicidal thoughts and behaviors in LGBTQ plus students. And so we know that the social environment, when it is

24
00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:48.000
supportive, has a positive impact on those young people. So I'm, this data doesn't tell us, But if you're asking me to, you know, what impact do I think the current conversations are having? It's impossible to suggest that they're not having a negative impact. Sure. And you probably can't tell this from the data either. But how can we as a society get help to this particular group of teens when so many politicians and others are making it impossible for educators and other people to speak openly about LGBTQ issues in schools? You know, it's certainly not my role from the federal perspective to, you know, advocate for or against any particular state law or policy. That's not my role.

25
00:18:48.000 --> 00:19:40.000
But our research has shown that when you make schools, for instance, less toxic for LGBTQ plus students. So when you put policies and practices in place in schools to support those young. people, not only do young people who identify as lesbian, gay, bisexual, or say that they're questioning do better in terms of their mental health and their, and suicidal thoughts and behavior, but straight youth in those schools, the youth who identify as heterosexual in those same schools also have better mental health and less suicidal thoughts and behaviors. And so what we see is that when you make schools less toxic for the most vulnerable youth, you make schools less toxic and more supportive for everyone.

26
00:19:40.000 --> 00:20:15.000
And so, you know, I, you know, of course, when you have a group of young people who are fearing significantly worse than others, you want to, you know, you want to talk about all the ways in which they can be supported and you can, you know, in the ways in which you can try to turn that around. But interestingly, supporting them. those young people also helps everybody. And in an environment that we're seeing in our data where no one's doing particularly well to make such important environments more toxic for any group of young people,

27
00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:57.000
understanding the way that it affects everyone, I think, worries me. Now, I want to stress that the recent report on youth risk behavior was not all bad news, right? I mean, there were some areas where youth did better than on prior service. Can you talk about those and some of the possible reasons for those improvements? Yeah. So we have, we've been consistently over, you know, the last three waves of this data when we looked at these 10-year trends. We've been seeing sexual behaviors move in the right direction for a while. And interestingly, you know, starting so, you know, we looked at 2007 to 2017 and then then 2,000 and then 2,000 and.

28
00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:49.000
9 to 2019, and now we're looking at 2011 to 2021. At the beginning of that time period, that 2011 time period, there were significant differences by race ethnicity in the proportion of youth who had ever had sex, who had multiple sex partners who were currently sexually active. Not only has everybody improved, but those differences, those disparities have closed. And now there aren't significant differences between those groups and the way that. that we used to see. We don't know exactly why that's occurring. I think we've spent a lot of time kind of, you know, educating young people about the positive behaviors that we think will be healthy for them. And so they listen. And we've been seeing the same thing across substance use as well.

29
00:21:49.000 --> 00:22:37.000
So even though we see this disparity between girls and boys, in general, substance use has been going down across all of the measures that we included in this report. And again, they're not all the measures, you know, contained in Y or BS, but across this set that we've been looking at for a while, they've all moved in the right direction. So I think that those are positive things in the sense that when you give young people information about their health and skills to make those changes, they are able to do that. What the areas that we're seeing that are, not positive, are the things that are happening to them, like the experience of violence, and then the resulting mental health impact of those experiences.

30
00:22:37.000 --> 00:23:24.000
Now, your division at the CDC works on resources for schools to help address violence and mental health. Can you tell us more about how schools can help teens and what exactly it is that the CDC is doing in this area? Sure. So specifically about schools. So again, my division's the division of adolescent in school health. We have a program called What Works in Schools. And what that program does is it works to improve health education, so making sure that schools are putting in place quality health education programs. It works to help connect young people in their schools to needed health services. And then it does a collection of what we call safe and supportive environment work.

31
00:23:24.000 --> 00:24:33.000
And that is everything from, making sure teachers have the training and the skills that they need to help manage the mental and behavioral health issues that they're seeing in their classrooms, they're telling us all the time that they're dealing with, especially since the pandemic, making sure that their youth development programs in place, which are really effective in helping young people feel engaged and connected to important others in their schools and to important adults. And then this set of things that, you know, I was just talking about in terms of making sure that schools are put in place policies and practices to support LGBTQ youth. When schools do all of that work, what we see is that in the same district, if you look at schools that put those programs in place versus schools that don't, even in the same district, that the schools that put the program in place, we see decreases in sexual behavior, we see decreases in substance use. We see decreases in both forced sex and in the proportion of young people who say they didn't go to school because they were worried about their safety. So it improves

32
00:24:33.000 --> 00:25:11.000
kind of the safety of the school environment. And particularly in the schools that put the LGBTQ inclusivity policies and practices in place, that's really where we see the positive impact on mental health and decreases in suicidality for both LGBT. LGBTQ youth and heterosexual youth. And the CDC makes these policies and practices available to what all schools in America, all public schools, public and private. I mean, can any school, and if a parent says where my kid goes, the school isn't doing it, how can they get it implemented?

33
00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:49.000
Sure. So we currently fund 28 large urban school districts to put the What Works and Schools program in place. we are very excited that what works in schools was included in the budget and brief that was released by the White House yesterday for expansion. So we're really excited about that. It's part of the behavioral health transformation initiative and we're just thrilled about that. But we put the all of the ways in which we work with school. So all of the things that we recommend that schools do,

34
00:25:49.000 --> 00:26:27.000
are available on our website. The What Works and Schools program is described on our website. We make our program guidance available to anybody who would like to use it. So that kind of gives you the play by play of all the things that we recommend that you do. And so, you know, we're working on scaling it up because it is so effective in addressing some of the exact things that we're concerned about in the data that we see. And we're really excited about the possibility of being able to expand the program. Now, what about training teens to help each other? You know, there's been a lot of reporting on peer-to-peer interventions

35
00:26:27.000 --> 00:27:18.000
that could help teens recognize the signs of depression or suicide in their fellow teens. And I'm just wondering if there's a place in what you're doing for these kinds of programs. Yeah, I mean, I really think that that's a great technique. You know, the Division of Violence Prevention at CDC has some really wonderful bystander. intervention programs that they've been working on for schools that I think can help support the safety and supportiveness of school environments. I think those are really great kinds of approaches. We are very much working on how to engage young people more in their schools. So we currently have a project about how to help schools set up youth advisory boards. And I think, so I think both

36
00:27:18.000 --> 00:28:15.000
on the individual level in terms of helping young people recognize what's going on with their peers and supporting them and helping provide that pathway to important support, as well as engaging youth in hearing from them what they need in their schools are all really important approaches. We just last week had some presentations from young people from a couple of different states who are actually on their state boards of education. And I just thought that was the most wonderful approach. And these young people were incredible. They were so smart and so able to talk about what their schools needed. And so I was imagining what a great resource that they must be for the adults who are on their state boards of education. And I hope that more states decide to do that kind of

37
00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:56.000
work, but engaging young people in developing the solutions for the problems that we're seeing among them, right? Like, they're telling us they're in crisis. We should be asking them what they need. So for parents of teens who are concerned about what's happening with their kids right now, what can they do at home to recognize the signs of trouble and get support? And does the CDC have resources that parents could use? So a couple things. We have a lot of resources on some parenting approaches like parental monitoring, which is really a fancy name for know where your kids are,

38
00:28:56.000 --> 00:29:38.000
who they're with and what they're doing. And we know that that is really protective for young people. And I think what we also know about parental monitoring is that it involves parents really being engaged, right? They don't, they're not going to know where their teams are and what they're doing and who they're with unless they are asking the questions and unless their young people are telling them. And that and that requires that there be this kind of give and take and trust and relationship building. The other thing, you know, we've been, as we've been talking a lot about this data, I think one thing for that parents can do, and this starts early, right? This is not

39
00:29:38.000 --> 00:30:34.000
about waiting until your child is a teenager and starting this conversation. But I think parents can be starting with their young children to talk about how are you feeling? And giving them language, I feel happy. I feel sad. I feel angry. I feel frustrated. And to then engage with, okay, well, so what should we do about that? How, you know, what do we do with the happiness? What do we do when you feel sad? And, and, and, and, normalizing that conversation, that conversation so that you don't get to the teenage years and need to be trying to figure out how to talk to your teenager about their mental health. So I would really strongly encourage parents of young children to start those conversations early.

40
00:30:34.000 --> 00:31:32.000
I think that we know from kind of what we know about social and emotional learning, teaching children how to manage their emotions, how to negotiate through them, how to, you know, kind of understand what they are and work with those emotions is really important and can have, you know, really long-term impact. I will say for parents of teenagers, you can make sure that you're attending to changes in behavior, changes in appetite, changes in sleep patterns, anything that looks different in terms of the way that your teenager is acting. And to start those conversations if you haven't, if you haven't already, I think there are great resources across a whole variety of websites.

41
00:31:32.000 --> 00:32:28.000
and I would, you know, try to find out where services are in your, in your community. I know that's a huge problem for parents right now is finding services when they need them. So just kind of know that. Talk to your primary care provider. You know, hopefully they will have some ways for you to connect with local sources of services. So, you know, there's a whole kind of, there's a whole array of things you can do. I will say that our research shows that connect back to connectedness, that family connectedness, so that sense of bond, that sense of trust, that sense of closeness, family connectedness, has long-term implications for health and well-being, not just during adolescence, but well into

42
00:32:28.000 --> 00:33:18.000
adulthood. And so in the same way that we see that school connectedness has 20-year impacts on a whole wide variety of outcomes, family connectedness does as well. So the degree to which young people in 7 through 12th grade said that they felt close to and connected to their family, 20 years later, they have better mental health, they are less likely to be suicidal, they have better, they have better physical, they're less likely to use substances, all of those things. We see that similarly with school connectedness. And a lot of what we do in terms of creating safe and supportive school environments is meant to increase school connectedness. Family connectedness is just as important. So, you know, it may seem, it may seem for parents of teenagers

43
00:33:18.000 --> 00:34:04.000
that it's hard to make that connection, but they do want it. It may seem like they're pushing you away, but they do want to be connected to you. And those connections have, long-term impact. So keep trying. Keep trying. Just keep it up. Keep trying. It's easy for me to say. I don't have kids. So last question. What's next for your division's research and when will the results of the next youth risk behavior survey be available? So this is actually, this report is one teeny, tiny little piece of all of the data that we have available. And so, you know, we wanted, because, Because I think of the importance of it, we wanted to get it out as quickly as we could. And we wanted it to be accessible.

44
00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:40.000
And we wanted it to be, while scientifically sound, we wanted people to be able to look at it and read it and take it in. I think that's happened. So we're really excited about that. But then we release, there will be a set of morbidity and mortality weekly reports that will come out at the end of April that will detail other areas. Some of the same outcomes will be included in some of the reports. But I think there's nine papers. There will be a big overview paper if you're interested in the methodology of the YRBS, as well as kind of additional individual papers that look more at the interrelationships

45
00:34:40.000 --> 00:35:23.000
between some of these variables. And then right around that time, we will release all of the data. So both the national data and all of the state and local data will be available on youth online. That is the way that we make that data available. And so that will just open it up for anybody who's really interested in digging further into the data, we'll be able to do that. We have a number of papers that are in the works from this data. And I encourage anyone who really wants to dig in and look and do their own analyses. We have the way to provide public use data sets to folks who want them.

46
00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:43.000
and we'll be happy to do that. There's a link to that you can request those data sets. So for, again, all of your data-oriented listeners, they're there. And then we'll collect the 2020-3 survey is currently in the field. So that will be available probably in about a year and a half. Okay. All right. Well, that's very interesting.

